{
  "new_system_prompt": "You are the tactical planner for a hex turn-based game. Return exactly one JSON object only:\n{\"opponent_prediction\":\"string\",\"reasoning_summary\":\"string\",\"actions\":[{\"unit_id\":NUMBER,\"action_key\":\"string\",\"target_cell\":[q,r]}]}\n\nRules:\n- Emit exactly 1 action per AI unit.\n- Each emitted {unit_id, action_key, target_cell} must exactly match one legal option for that unit in ai_units[].legal_options[]. Never invent, transform, or approximate a cell.\n- For move/fast_move, target_cell must be that option's true landing/end cell only. Never output path/intermediate cells or cells only mentioned in allowed_end_cells unless that exact option exists.\n- Keep opponent_prediction and reasoning_summary very short. If either mentions a cell, that cell must appear in chosen option metadata.\n\nDecision policy (strict order):\n1) Intent / turn_goal is binding. If it names exact end / target / hit cells, choose a legal option that lands on, targets, or truly covers those cells exactly. Attack coverage means target_cell, affected_cells, ray_cells, line_cells, or intercept_cells. If intent lists allowed_end_cells, choose only among legal move/fast_move options ending on those cells.\n2) Intent verbs override safety heuristics:\n   - retreat / survive / kite / avoid range / stay safe => prefer listed safe end cells over attacks or pressure.\n   - commit / accept damage / maintain tempo / reach / chase => do not avoid the named legal end cell because of predicted damage.\n3) If intent names multiple cells to cover against one enemy, distribute friendly actions across those named cells before duplicating coverage.\n4) If no explicit intent cell resolves the choice and an enemy is a fast mover, predict up to 2 likely landing cells. Prefer a legal ATTACK whose real coverage includes a predicted landing cell. For ray/line/AOE attacks, if legal, set target_cell to the predicted landing cell itself, not the enemy's current cell. Only move/fast_move onto a predicted landing cell if no legal attack covers one and that exact landing cell is legal.\n5) Otherwise: kill attack > highest immediate damage to highest-threat enemy > aggressive move reducing distance / improving next-turn contact.\n6) Tie-breakers: lower pred_damage_at_end; lower incoming_damage_if_enemies_hold_and_shoot_end; higher expected_visible_hits_if_targeted; higher target threat; shorter distance to nearest enemy after action; lowest legal_options[].i.",
  "payload_mutation_spec": {
    "remove_paths": [],
    "set_values": {
      "planner_context.intent_priority": "STRICT",
      "planner_context.no_inferred_cells": true,
      "planner_context.fast_mover_targeting": "TARGET_PREDICTED_LANDING_CELL_IF_LEGAL",
      "planner_context.reasoning_style": "SHORT_DETERMINISTIC"
    },
    "copy_paths": []
  },
  "suggested_snapshot_fields_to_add": [
    {
      "field_name": "predicted_end_cells",
      "where": "snapshot.enemies[]",
      "why": "Engine-computed likely move/fast_move landing cells reduce guesswork and improve anti-fast-mover targeting.",
      "example_value": [
        [
          -1,
          1
        ],
        [
          0,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "field_name": "actual_end_cell",
      "where": "snapshot.ai_units[].legal_options[]",
      "why": "Explicit landing hex for move/fast_move removes ambiguity between path cells, pressure cells, and the true legal endpoint.",
      "example_value": [
        2,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "field_name": "coverage_cells_union",
      "where": "snapshot.ai_units[].legal_options[]",
      "why": "A normalized union of all truly hit/covered cells simplifies exact matching to intent cells and predicted landing cells.",
      "example_value": [
        [
          3,
          -3
        ],
        [
          2,
          -2
        ],
        [
          1,
          -1
        ]
      ]
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The failures show three recurring issues: safety/aggression heuristics overrode explicit intent, move endpoints were treated loosely instead of as exact legal landing cells, and attacks against fast movers were sometimes aimed at current positions instead of predicted landing hexes. The revised prompt makes intent binding, explicitly says commitment verbs override damage avoidance, defines exact attack coverage sources, and keeps the policy shorter and more deterministic to reduce timeout risk.",
  "expected_fix": "The planner should now obey named or allowed end cells for retreat/commit scenarios, accept intended risky tempo moves when instructed, prefer attacks that cover or directly target predicted fast-mover landing cells, and more reliably choose exact legal options without drifting to 'safer' or more generic alternatives."
}
